---
import ServiceLayout from '../layouts/ServiceLayout.astro';
import { faqs } from '../config/faqs';

const description =
	'Network segmentation for Colorado homes and small businesses. Trusted devices, guests, cameras, and smart equipment kept on the parts of the network they need.';
---

<ServiceLayout
	title="Network Segmentation & Security"
	description={description}
	eyebrow="Network security"
	heading="A smarter network is also a safer network."
	lede="Not every device needs to see every other device. Guests, cameras, laptops, and thermostats can share a building without sharing a single open network."
	crumb="Network Security"
	serviceName="Network segmentation and security"
	serviceType="Network security"
	faqs={faqs.security}
	ctaLabel="Get a Network Assessment"
	ctaTitle="Get a network assessment."
	ctaText="If guests, cameras, and work computers currently share one WiFi name, there is a clearer way to arrange it."
	related={[
		{ href: '/unifi-networking/', label: 'UniFi networking', text: 'The gateway and switches that make separation real.' },
		{ href: '/security-cameras/', label: 'Security cameras', text: 'Recorders belong on their own segment.' },
		{ href: '/wifi-design/', label: 'Professional WiFi', text: 'Each group still needs signal where the people are.' },
	]}
>
	<section class="section" aria-labelledby="problem-heading">
		<div class="wrap split">
			<div>
				<h2 id="problem-heading">One password for everything is a single room.</h2>
			</div>
			<div class="prose">
				<p>
					When a guest phone, a smart TV, a camera, and a work laptop all sit on the same network, a problem on the
					least careful device has a short walk to the others. Flat networks are easy to set up. They are also easy
					to cross.
				</p>
				<p>
					Segmentation is the practice of giving each group the access it needs and refusing the rest. The result
					is quieter daily life, not a new set of rules for the people in the house.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section section-cream" aria-labelledby="groups-heading">
		<div class="wrap">
			<h2 id="groups-heading">A practical arrangement.</h2>
			<p class="lede">Names vary by property. The separation is the point.</p>
			<ul class="index-list">
				<li>
					<strong>Trusted network</strong>
					<p>Computers, phones, and servers. The devices that hold work and personal files.</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>IoT network</strong>
					<p>Smart TVs, automation gear, thermostats, and other devices that need the internet more than they need your laptop.</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Security network</strong>
					<p>Cameras and recording equipment, reachable for viewing and management, not wandering the rest of the building.</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Guest network</strong>
					<p>Internet access for visitors, without a route to private devices, printers, or cameras.</p>
				</li>
			</ul>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section" aria-labelledby="vlan-heading">
		<div class="wrap split">
			<div>
				<h2 id="vlan-heading">VLANs and firewall rules, without the jargon first.</h2>
			</div>
			<div class="prose">
				<p>
					A VLAN divides one physical network into separate logical ones. The same switches and cables can carry
					several groups that do not automatically trust each other. Firewall rules then decide the exceptions: who
					may reach a printer, who may only reach the internet, who may view a camera.
				</p>
				<p>
					You do not have to remember the acronym. You should be able to say, in a sentence, which devices are
					allowed to talk. The configuration is documented so that sentence remains true after the install.
				</p>
				<p>
					Nothing here is a claim that a segmented network is immune to attack. It is a claim that unnecessary
					paths have been closed.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>
</ServiceLayout>
